{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And the land shall not be sold in perpetuity</b> — to establish a prohibition against the return of fields to their owners in the Jubilee, so that the purchaser should not retain permanent hold of it; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:23). But if so, why does Scripture issue the warning regarding the sale, when the appropriate formulation would be \"you shall not acquire in perpetuity\"? Perhaps one should read it as: it shall not be sold by you in perpetuity. And similarly, \"they shall not be sold as a slave is sold\" (Leviticus 25:42) — a warning to the purchaser that he must release him in the Jubilee, according to its plain sense. And it is possible that \"it shall not be sold in perpetuity\" is a prohibition upon the seller, that he should not sell it as an outright conveyance, saying: I hereby sell it to you forever, even after the Jubilee. For even though the Jubilee voids it, Scripture warned the seller — or both of them — that they should not make their sale in perpetuity, and if they say so they transgress this prohibition, yet it avails them nothing, for it reverts in the Jubilee. And thus the Rav, Rabbi Moses, explained it (Hilkhot Shemittah ve-Yovel, chapter 11, halakhah 1). And the reason for this is that it is well known in the thinking of people that if they make their sale from the outset for a set number of years until the Jubilee, the matter seems light in their eyes; whereas if it is acquired as an outright conveyance, the return becomes very difficult in their eyes. And it will be like the matter they said (Temurah 4a): What does the Merciful One mean by \"you shall not work\"? If he does work it, it is of no avail, and he is lashed because he transgressed the decree of the King. And what seems correct in my eyes is that this is not a prohibition for which one receives lashes, but rather it is a reason: he says, conduct the Jubilee among yourselves and do not find it difficult, \"for the land is Mine,\" and I do not wish it to be sold in perpetuity like other sales. And this is their intent in Torat Kohanim (chapter 4, section 8): \"In perpetuity\" — as an outright conveyance. \"For the land is Mine\" — do not begrudge it. \"For you are strangers and settlers\" — do not regard yourselves as the principal. \"You are with Me\" — it is enough for the servant to be like his master: since it is Mine, it is yours. And the reason of \"for the land is Mine\" by way of truth, is like \"and they shall take for Me an offering\" (Exodus 25:2), and this is what they alluded to here: it is enough for the servant to be like his master, for the Jubilee will obtain in the world. And the enlightened will understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The citation from Temurah 4a abbreviates the Gemara's discussion; the phrase אַהֻרְמָנָא דְּמַלְכָּא ('decree of the King') is rendered literally as in the original Aramaic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:26.737906943Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}